th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","history_only":true,"curation_sha256":"402117cd2ecc28eaf6387aaefcadec5f0c0540f0912f8a96983845daf37ec8e2","concept_id":"Year-2/2b-Game-Theory/expected-payoff-maximization.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-2/2b-Game-Theory/expected-payoff-maximization.html","questions":[{"id":"year_2_2b_game_theory_expected_payoff_maximization_11753ff7_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A decision maker chooses an action $a\\in A$; each action induces a probability distribution over outcomes $x$, and $v(x)$ is the payoff of outcome $x$. What does choosing an action determine in such a decision problem?","options":["The outcome itself, since the action fixes what happens","The payoff function $v$, since each action has its own $v$","The set of possible outcomes, but not their probabilities","The probability distribution of the outcome, not the outcome itself"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5a94d7e0b7e6149015775053","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"year_2_2b_game_theory_expected_payoff_maximization_11753ff7_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Under an action the outcome $x$ is uniform on $[0,6]$, with density $1/6$, and the payoff is $v(x)=x^2$. What is the expected payoff $\\mathbb{E}[v(x)\\mid a]$?","options":["$12$","$9$","$72$","$36$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"756d674533c45a4f09f811d6","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"year_2_2b_game_theory_expected_payoff_maximization_11753ff7_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"A decision maker chooses an action $a\\in A$; each action induces a probability distribution over outcomes $x$, and $v(x)$ is the payoff of outcome $x$. Lotteries $L_1$ and $L_2$ have expected payoffs $E_1\\le E_2$. A compound lottery runs $L_1$ with probability $\\lambda\\in[0,1]$ and $L_2$ otherwise. Can it give a strictly higher expected payoff than both $L_1$ and $L_2$?","options":["No: its expected payoff $\\lambda E_1+(1-\\lambda)E_2$ is at most $E_2$.","Yes: combining lotteries spreads risk and raises the expected payoff.","Yes, if $\\lambda=\\tfrac{1}{2}$ and the lotteries have different outcomes.","No, but only because $E_1$ and $E_2$ are assumed equal."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f9578c778ad14c577774579a","time_limit":55,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"}]}}